  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What does iron need to rust?",
      "a": "Both water and oxygen. Iron in dry air or in water with no oxygen hardly rusts at all.",
      "asks": [
        "What causes rust?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why does salt make rust happen faster?",
      "a": "Rusting works like a tiny battery, with electric charge moving through the water. Salty water conducts electricity much better, so the reaction runs faster.",
      "asks": [
        "Why do cars rust near the sea?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why does rust keep spreading?",
      "a": "Rust is crumbly and flaky, takes up more space than the iron, and falls away, so fresh iron underneath is exposed and starts rusting too.",
      "asks": [
        "Why doesn't rust protect iron?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How can you stop iron rusting?",
      "a": "Keep water and oxygen off it with paint, oil, grease or plastic, or coat it in zinc, called galvanising. Stainless steel contains chromium, which forms a thin protective layer.",
      "asks": [
        "How do you prevent rust?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Do other metals rust?",
      "a": "Strictly, only iron and steel rust. Other metals corrode in their own ways: copper turns green, silver goes black, and aluminium forms a thin protective coat.",
      "asks": [
        "Does aluminium rust?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "Rust is mainly hydrated iron(III) oxide, formed when iron reacts with oxygen and water.",
    "Galvanised steel is coated in zinc, which corrodes first and protects the iron underneath."
  ],
